{"name":"Flickering Firefly","hex":"#f8f6e6","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"flickering","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["shining unsteadily or varying rapidly in brightness (referring to a light or a source of light)"]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["present participle and gerund of flicker"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/flickering"},{"term":"firefly","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any beetle of the family Lampyridae, which exhibit bioluminescence during twilight."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/firefly"}],"entries":[{"title":"Firefly","blocks":[{"text":"The Lampyridae are a family of elateroid beetles with more than 2,400 described species, many of which are light-emitting. They are soft-bodied beetles commonly called fireflies, lightning bugs, or glowworms for their conspicuous emission of light, mainly during twilight, to attract mates. The type species is Lampyris noctiluca, the common glow-worm of Europe. Light production in the Lampyridae is thought to have originated as a warning signal that the larvae were distasteful. This ability to create light was then co-opted as a mating signal and, in a further development, adult female fireflies of the genus Photuris mimic the flash pattern of the Photinus beetle to trap their males as prey."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/52/Photuris_lucicrescens.jpg/330px-Photuris_lucicrescens.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Firefly"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Lampyris_Noctiluca_%28firefly%29_mating.gif/500px-Lampyris_Noctiluca_%28firefly%29_mating.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Lampyris_Noctiluca_%28firefly%29_mating.gif/500px-Lampyris_Noctiluca_%28firefly%29_mati